{
 "summary": "Adds checks during transaction deserialization to reject transactions that could never be valid. Transactions claiming more inputs or outputs than could possibly fit in a block are rejected everywhere, bounding vector allocation sizes. Additionally, transactions with zero inputs or zero outputs are rejected early in P2P message deserialization via a new serializer parameter.",
 "problem": "Nodes fully deserialize transactions and blocks before passing them to validation, exposing nodes to worst-case memory allocation. A malicious peer can send a 4 MB block containing roughly 400,000 empty transactions (10 bytes each) that each allocate a full CTransaction object, or transactions specifying huge vector counts that trigger repeated multi-megabyte reallocations.",
 "evidence": [
  "Commit 82cd7e7427: 'This reduces the memory a peer can make us allocate by sending a block full of such transactions. At 10 bytes each, about 400000 of them fit in a block message, each of which gets its own CTransaction.'",
  "Commit ec2cabb795: 'This allows us to allocate the vin and vout vectors immediately, rather than sequentially in batches of 5 MB as the std::vector deserializer does, because that memory becomes bounded: to 2.7 MB for 24390 inputs, and 5.3 MB for 111111 outputs.'"
 ],
 "dependencies": {
  "depends_on": [],
  "enables": [
   "#36895"
  ]
 },
 "scope_notes": "Changes P2P behavior slightly: blocks and transactions with zero inputs or outputs or excessive input/output counts are now treated as stream deserialization errors (the message is dropped/ignored) rather than passing to validation where an invalid block would cause peer disconnection or an invalid transaction would enter the reject filter.",
 "changed_since_previous": "Dropped cumulative block weight tracking during deserialization and the accompanying mutable stream parameter changes, leaving only the per-transaction input/output count limits and the P2P rejection of empty inputs or outputs.",
 "card": "This PR adds early checks during transaction deserialization to prevent malicious peers from exploiting worst-case memory allocations. It globally rejects transactions specifying more inputs or outputs than can fit in a block, allowing immediate vector capacity reservation bounded to at most 5.3 MB. It also rejects transactions with zero inputs or zero outputs across P2P message handling, preventing peers from flooding a node with up to 400,000 10-byte empty transactions in a single 4 MB block. As a behavioral change, these malformed transactions and blocks are ignored as deserialization failures rather than being rejected in validation and triggering peer disconnection.",
